    "conclusion": "YES - the quantum channel IS the database's own value for this subset, re-serialised at lower precision. Among 331 database-matched bond observations: |db - quantum| has median 0.0195 kcal/mol (mean 0.158, max 6.999); 30.2% within 0.01, 81.0% within 0.1, 92.4% within 0.5, and 100.0% within 1.0 kcal/mol. For the learned channel the same comparison gives median 0.1291 (6.6x worse), 5.1% within 0.01, 42.3% within 0.1, 92.4% within 0.5, and 97.6% within 1.0 (max 4.768). The learned channel therefore DISAGREES: it is a fitted approximation to the same target, not a copy.",
    "direction_of_evidence": "Paired test on the same 331 bond observations: quantum is closer to the database row than learned on 243 rows vs 76 (Wilcoxon p=5.8e-17). Both channels agree with the database to 0.001 kcal/mol on some rows (quantum 7/331), which fixes the database row as their common underlying source; neither is bit-identical because both are stored rounded.",
    "residual_structure": "The 25/331 rows with |db-quantum| > 0.5 are dominated by hydrogen-transfer observations (e.g. cyclodecane bond_observation_0086: db 94.772 vs quantum 92.60, 20 database rows). The database stores one value per symmetry-equivalent key while the quantum channel stores an explicitly evaluated geometry. The residual is mean-zero (mean +0.0192, sd 0.582), so it is not a systematic offset - it is a per-geometry sampling difference inside the same source value."
